<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Congress</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1789">1789</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Congress of the United States</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Congress is the legislature of the United States, made up of the Senate, with two members from each state, and the House of Representatives, elected by population. Created by the Constitution, it first met in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a> in March 1789, succeeding the Congress of the Confederation. It makes federal law, levies taxes, declares war and confirms treaties and appointments, and it meets in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> in Washington.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-congress.jpg" alt="United States Congress" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Martin Falbisoner · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:US_Capitol_west_side.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
